  "summary": "Recent work moves beyond raw neural signals toward inferred structured objects, from fMRI combinatorial complexes to network control of timescales.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is inference of a structured object (a combinatorial complex, a latent manifold, or a network-control model) rather than direct analysis of raw neural activity. One preprint builds data-driven combinatorial complexes from fMRI using O-information and S-information to capture higher-order synergistic interactions. A second preprint interprets a neural foundation model of visual neural activity through decoding and encoding manifolds, tracking how encoder, recurrent, and readout components develop. The published study applies network control theory to connectome structure to estimate regional intrinsic neural timescales, validated against fMRI timescales and gene expression. What is new is treating higher-order topology as a modeling primitive and linking structural connectome constraints to dynamical timescale estimates. The manifold analysis is representational rather than graph-based, so the dominant thread is structured representation rather than any single graph method.",
